  "content": "<img src=\"[hero-image-url-paste-here]\" alt=\"Common Tense Errors in SSC CGL and Bank PO\" style=\"width:100%;border-radius:8px;\" />\n\n<h2>You Already Know the Rule. You Still Got It Wrong.</h2>\n<p>You studied tenses for months. You still marked the wrong option in the mock test. Sound familiar? <strong>Common tense errors in SSC CGL and Bank PO</strong> exams don't happen because you don't know grammar. They happen because exam sentences are built to trick your eye, not test your memory. In the next few minutes, you'll see the exact 20 sentence patterns that trip up most aspirants, and the simple checks that stop the bleeding.</p>\n\n<div style=\"background:#f0f7ff;border-left:4px solid #0066cc;padding:15px;margin:20px 0;\">\n<p>Tense errors in SSC CGL and Bank PO exams usually come from mixing time markers like 'since' and 'for', stacking two tense auxiliaries in one verb, or forgetting to backshift verbs in reported speech. Spotting the time clue in the sentence first, before reading the options, fixes most of these errors.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Table of Contents</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><a href=\"#section-3\">Why Tense Errors Keep Costing You Marks</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#section-4\">5 Tense Mistakes Aspirants Repeat Every Year</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#section-5\">6-Step Strategy to Catch Tense Errors Fast</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#section-6\">How Ravi Turned This Around in One Month</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#section-7\">Expert Tips From 15+ Years of Coaching</a></li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2 id=\"section-3\">Why Tense Errors Keep Costing You Marks</h2>\n<p>Here's the part nobody tells you in class. Error-spotting questions aren't testing whether you know the twelve tenses. They're testing whether you can catch a wrong tense buried inside a long, boring sentence, in under 40 seconds. That's a different skill.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>You read fast under exam pressure and miss the time-marker word hiding in the middle of the sentence.</li>\n<li>You memorised tense charts but never practised spotting errors in full-length Mains-style sentences.</li>\n<li>You confuse 'since' and 'for' every single time, and the exam knows it.</li>\n<li>You get reported speech questions and forget the verb has to shift back one tense.</li>\n<li>You panic, second-guess a correct sentence, and mark 'No error' as wrong anyway.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>I know exactly how that feels. You walk out of the exam hall replaying the sentence in your head, realising the mistake thirty seconds too late. But here's what most people get wrong: they keep practising more questions instead of fixing the pattern behind the mistakes.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"section-4\">5 Tense Mistakes Aspirants Repeat Every Year</h2>\n<p>These aren't invented examples. They're the patterns that show up again and again in SSC CGL and Bank PO papers, in one form or another.</p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Mixing up 'since' and 'for'</strong><br>This is the single most repeated tense trap in error spotting. 'Since' points to a starting time. 'For' points to a duration. Exams love hiding this one inside a long sentence about experiments, jobs, or years of experience.<br>Wrong: These experiments had been going on since several months.<br>Correct: These experiments had been going on for several months.<br><em>Source: SSC CGL Mains – 29/01/2022</em></p>\n\n<p><strong>2. Stacking two tense auxiliaries in one verb</strong><br>Some sentences try to combine future and perfect tense wrongly, creating a verb phrase that simply doesn't exist in English. Your ear should catch this before your brain does.<br>Wrong: We will have been know the result of the CAT exam by next week.<br>Correct: We will know the result of the CAT exam by next week.<br><em>Source: Common exam pattern (SSC CGL)</em></p>\n\n<p><strong>3. Present tense verb in a clearly past-tense sentence</strong><br>A time word like 'yesterday' or 'last week' locks the whole sentence into simple past. If the verb doesn't match, that's your error.<br>Wrong: I go to the store yesterday.<br>Correct: I went to the store yesterday.<br><em>Source: Common exam pattern (SSC CGL / Bank PO)</em></p>\n\n<p><strong>4. Wrong auxiliary in present perfect ('have being')</strong><br>Present perfect needs 'have/has' plus the third form of the verb, never 'have being'. This mistake sneaks in because 'being' looks grammatical at first glance.<br>Wrong: We have being arranged everything for the meeting.<br>Correct: We have arranged everything for the meeting.<br><em>Source: Common exam pattern (SSC CGL)</em></p>\n\n<p><strong>5. Forgetting to backshift tense in reported speech</strong><br>When you report what someone said, the tense usually moves one step back. Aspirants often copy the original tense instead.<br>Wrong: She said that she is coming tomorrow.<br>Correct: She said that she was coming the next day.<br><em>Source: Common exam pattern (SSC CGL / IBPS PO)</em></p>\n\n<div style=\"max-width:700px;margin:30px auto;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;\">\n  <h3 style=\"background:#1B3A6B;color:#F5A623;padding:14px 20px;border-radius:8px 8px 0 0;margin:0;font-size:18px;text-align:center;\">\n    Common Mistakes Students Make\n  </h3>\n  <div style=\"border:2px solid #1B3A6B;border-top:none;border-radius:0 0 8px 8px;overflow:hidden;\">\n\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #e0e8f5;background:#fff;\">\n      <div style=\"font-size:22px;margin-right:14px;margin-top:2px;\">&#10060;</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Mistake 1: Mixing up 'since' and 'for'</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">'Since' marks a starting point in time; 'for' marks a duration.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;color:#c0392b;font-size:13px;font-style:italic;\">Wrong: These experiments had been going on since several months.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#27ae60;font-size:13px;font-style:italic;\">Correct: These experiments had been going on for several months.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #e0e8f5;background:#fff;\">\n      <div style=\"font-size:22px;margin-right:14px;margin-top:2px;\">&#10060;</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Mistake 2: Stacking two tense auxiliaries</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">A verb phrase should never combine 'will have been' with a base verb like 'know'.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;color:#c0392b;font-size:13px;font-style:italic;\">Wrong: We will have been know the result of the CAT exam by next week.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#27ae60;font-size:13px;font-style:italic;\">Correct: We will know the result of the CAT exam by next week.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #e0e8f5;background:#fff;\">\n      <div style=\"font-size:22px;margin-right:14px;margin-top:2px;\">&#10060;</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Mistake 3: Present verb in a past-tense sentence</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">A time word like 'yesterday' locks the sentence into simple past.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;color:#c0392b;font-size:13px;font-style:italic;\">Wrong: I go to the store yesterday.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#27ae60;font-size:13px;font-style:italic;\">Correct: I went to the store yesterday.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #e0e8f5;background:#fff;\">\n      <div style=\"font-size:22px;margin-right:14px;margin-top:2px;\">&#10060;</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Mistake 4: Wrong auxiliary in present perfect</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">Present perfect needs 'have/has' plus the third form, never 'have being'.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;color:#c0392b;font-size:13px;font-style:italic;\">Wrong: We have being arranged everything for the meeting.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#27ae60;font-size:13px;font-style:italic;\">Correct: We have arranged everything for the meeting.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;background:#fff;\">\n      <div style=\"font-size:22px;margin-right:14px;margin-top:2px;\">&#10060;</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Mistake 5: Forgetting to backshift in reported speech</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">When reporting speech, the tense usually moves one step back.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;color:#c0392b;font-size:13px;font-style:italic;\">Wrong: She said that she is coming tomorrow.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#27ae60;font-size:13px;font-style:italic;\">Correct: She said that she was coming the next day.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n  </div>\n  <p style=\"text-align:center;font-size:12px;color:#888;margin-top:8px;\">\n    ebookcharm — English Grammar Made Exam-Ready\n  </p>\n</div>\n\n<h2 id=\"section-5\">6-Step Strategy to Catch Tense Errors Fast</h2>\n<p>You don't need to re-learn grammar from scratch. You need a system you can run in your head in under a minute per sentence.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1: Find the time marker first.</strong> Words like 'since', 'yesterday', 'by next week', or 'already' tell you which tense the sentence is supposed to be in, before you even look at the verb.<br>Example: She is working here since 2019 → should be 'has been working since 2019'.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: Match the subject to the correct auxiliary.</strong> Collective nouns like 'team', 'committee', or 'jury' usually take a singular auxiliary in these exams.<br>Example: The team have submitted their report → should be 'The team has submitted its report'.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: Never allow two tense auxiliaries in one verb phrase.</strong> If a sentence has 'will have been' followed by a base verb like 'know', stop and re-check it.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4: Apply the 'since vs for' rule every single time you see either word.</strong> Make this an automatic reflex, not a decision you make under pressure.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5: In reported speech, shift the tense one step back.</strong> Present becomes past, present perfect becomes past perfect, and 'tomorrow' becomes 'the next day'.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 6: Read the entire sentence before you touch an option.</strong> Many aspirants lock onto the first odd-looking word and miss a real error later in the sentence.</p>\n\n<div style=\"max-width:700px;margin:30px auto;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;\">\n  <h3 style=\"background:#1B3A6B;color:#F5A623;padding:14px 20px;border-radius:8px 8px 0 0;margin:0;font-size:18px;text-align:center;\">\n    Step-by-Step Strategy\n  </h3>\n  <div style=\"border:2px solid #1B3A6B;border-top:none;border-radius:0 0 8px 8px;padding:10px 0;\">\n\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #e0e8f5;\">\n      <div style=\"min-width:36px;height:36px;background:#F5A623;color:#1B3A6B;border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:bold;font-size:16px;margin-right:16px;margin-top:2px;flex-shrink:0;\">1</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Find the time marker first</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">Words like 'since', 'yesterday', or 'by next week' tell you the correct tense before you check the verb.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;background:#f0f4fb;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;font-size:13px;color:#1B3A6B;font-style:italic;\">Example: She is working here since 2019 → has been working since 2019.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #e0e8f5;\">\n      <div style=\"min-width:36px;height:36px;background:#F5A623;color:#1B3A6B;border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:bold;font-size:16px;margin-right:16px;margin-top:2px;flex-shrink:0;\">2</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Match the subject to the correct auxiliary</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">Collective nouns like 'team' or 'committee' usually take a singular auxiliary in these exams.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;background:#f0f4fb;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;font-size:13px;color:#1B3A6B;font-style:italic;\">Example: The team have submitted their report → The team has submitted its report.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #e0e8f5;\">\n      <div style=\"min-width:36px;height:36px;background:#F5A623;color:#1B3A6B;border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:bold;font-size:16px;margin-right:16px;margin-top:2px;flex-shrink:0;\">3</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Never allow two tense auxiliaries in one verb</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">A phrase like 'will have been know' is always wrong. Stop and re-check it.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;background:#f0f4fb;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;font-size:13px;color:#1B3A6B;font-style:italic;\">Example: We will have been know the result → We will know the result.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #e0e8f5;\">\n      <div style=\"min-width:36px;height:36px;background:#F5A623;color:#1B3A6B;border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:bold;font-size:16px;margin-right:16px;margin-top:2px;flex-shrink:0;\">4</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Apply the 'since vs for' rule every time</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">Make this an automatic reflex rather than a decision you make under time pressure.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;background:#f0f4fb;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;font-size:13px;color:#1B3A6B;font-style:italic;\">Example: since several months → for several months.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid #e0e8f5;\">\n      <div style=\"min-width:36px;height:36px;background:#F5A623;color:#1B3A6B;border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:bold;font-size:16px;margin-right:16px;margin-top:2px;flex-shrink:0;\">5</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Backshift tense in reported speech</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">Present becomes past, and 'tomorrow' becomes 'the next day', when you report what someone said.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;background:#f0f4fb;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;font-size:13px;color:#1B3A6B;font-style:italic;\">Example: She said she is coming tomorrow → She said she was coming the next day.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;padding:14px 18px;\">\n      <div style=\"min-width:36px;height:36px;background:#F5A623;color:#1B3A6B;border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:bold;font-size:16px;margin-right:16px;margin-top:2px;flex-shrink:0;\">6</div>\n      <div>\n        <strong style=\"color:#1B3A6B;font-size:15px;\">Read the full sentence before marking an answer</strong>\n        <p style=\"margin:4px 0 0;color:#444;font-size:14px;\">Don't lock onto the first odd word — a real error can sit later in the sentence.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin:6px 0 0;background:#f0f4fb;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;font-size:13px;color:#1B3A6B;font-style:italic;\">Example: Re-read the whole sentence once, silently, before choosing an option.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n  </div>\n  <p style=\"text-align:center;font-size:12px;color:#888;margin-top:8px;\">\n    ebookcharm — English Grammar Made Exam-Ready\n  </p>\n</div>\n\n<h2 id=\"section-6\">How Ravi Turned This Around in One Month</h2>\n<p>Ravi, an SSC CGL aspirant from Nashik, was losing three to four marks on English every mock test, almost all of it on tense-based error spotting. He wasn't weak in grammar. He was weak in pattern recognition under time pressure. For four weeks, he picked one tense trap a day, wrote it on a flashcard with the wrong and correct version side by side, and reviewed ten cards before every mock test. By the end of the month, his error-spotting accuracy on tense questions had nearly doubled, and English stopped being the section that dragged his score down. You can do the same — here's how to start.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"section-7\">Expert Tips From 15+ Years of Coaching</h2>\n<p><strong>Tip 1: Treat time markers like a compass, not decoration.</strong> Every sentence with 'since', 'for', 'yet', 'already', or a specific date is telling you exactly which tense is correct. Most aspirants read past these words instead of anchoring on them.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip 2: Read the sentence aloud in your head, slowly, once.</strong> Wrong tense combinations often 'sound' off to a trained ear even before you can name the rule being broken.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip 3: Keep a personal 'tense trap' journal.</strong> Every time you get a tense question wrong in practice, write the sentence down. Most aspirants repeat the same three or four tense traps for months without realising it.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip 4: Practise on long, Mains-style sentences, not just short filler ones.</strong> Short practice sentences build false confidence. The real exam hides errors inside longer, more complex sentences, and that's exactly where you need the practice.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"section-8\">Your Next Step</h2>\n<p>Tense errors aren't a sign that you're bad at English. They're a sign that you've been practising the wrong way, on the wrong kind of sentences. Fix the pattern, and the marks come back on their own.</p>\n\n<div style=\"background:#fff8e1;border:2px solid #ffa000;padding:20px;border-radius:8px;text-align:center;margin:30px 0;\">\n<img src=\"[ebook-cover-url]\" alt=\"The Tense In English Grammar - A Practical Grammar Guide by Balu Kandekar\" style=\"max-width:200px;margin-bottom:15px;\" />\n<h3>Stop Losing Marks to the Same Tense Traps</h3>\n<p>'The Tense In English Grammar: A Practical Grammar Guide' walks you through every exam-relevant tense rule with real practice sentences, for just $2.99.</p>\n<a href=\"https://amzn.to/4bZN2gE\" style=\"background:#0066cc;color:white;padding:12px 24px;text-decoration:none;border-radius:5px;display:inline-block;\">Grab Your Copy →</a>\n</div>\n\n<h2 id=\"section-10\">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<div style=\"margin-bottom:15px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;padding-bottom:10px;\">\n<h4>Q: What is the most common tense error in SSC CGL exams?</h4>\n<p>A: Confusing 'since' and 'for' is by far the most repeated tense trap in SSC CGL error spotting, followed by wrong auxiliary use in present perfect sentences.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"margin-bottom:15px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;padding-bottom:10px;\">\n<h4>Q: How many marks does grammar carry in SSC CGL Tier 1?</h4>\n<p>A: English Comprehension, which includes error spotting and tense-based questions, typically carries 25 marks in SSC CGL Tier 1, so a handful of avoidable tense mistakes can meaningfully change your score.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"margin-bottom:15px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;padding-bottom:10px;\">\n<h4>Q: Is 'since' always used with present perfect tense?</h4>\n<p>A: Usually yes. 'Since' points to a fixed starting time, so it pairs with present perfect or present perfect continuous, while 'for' pairs with a duration and can appear with several tenses.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"margin-bottom:15px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;padding-bottom:10px;\">\n<h4>Q: Can simple past and present perfect appear correctly in the same sentence?</h4>\n<p>A: Yes, when each clause has its own clear time reference, but exams often test whether you can tell when mixing them is actually wrong.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"margin-bottom:15px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;padding-bottom:10px;\">\n<h4>Q: Is a $2.99 eBook worth it when there are free YouTube videos?</h4>\n<p>A: Free videos are scattered across hours of content. This guide compresses the exact exam-relevant tense rules into a short, focused resource you can revise in a single sitting before your exam.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"margin-bottom:15px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;padding-bottom:10px;\">\n<h4>Q: Will this help with IBPS PO along with SSC CGL?</h4>\n<p>A: Yes. Tense error patterns overlap heavily across SSC CGL, IBPS PO, RRB NTPC, and similar competitive exams, so the same rules apply directly.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"margin-bottom:15px;border-bottom:1px solid #eee;padding-bottom:10px;\">\n<h4>Q: What are the main tenses tested in competitive exam sentence correction?</h4>\n<p>A: Simple present, simple past, present perfect, present perfect continuous, past perfect, and future tense account for most sentence correction and error spotting questions in these exams.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Related Posts</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>📌 <a href=\"#\">Infinitive vs Gerund vs Participle</a> — clear up the confusion in five minutes</li>\n<li>📌 <a href=\"#\">Subject-Verb Agreement Rules for SSC CGL</a> — the other error-spotting favourite</li>\n<li>📌 <a href=\"#\">Active vs Passive Voice Made Simple</a> — spot voice errors just as fast</li>\n<li>📌 <a href=\"#\">Direct and Indirect Speech: Complete Guide</a> — master the backshift rule fully</li>\n</ul>"
